<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qos"><span class="enc-name">Qos</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Qos was the national god of the Edomites, worshipped in the Iron Age in the lands south-east of the Dead Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qu-wentai"><span class="enc-name">Qu Wentai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qu Wentai was king of Gaochang, the oasis kingdom of Turpan on the Silk Road, from 620.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qu-wuchen"><span class="enc-name">Qu Wuchen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qu Wuchen, also called Wuchen of Shen, was a noble of the State of Chu who fled to the State of Jin around 589 BCE, taking with him the famous beauty Xia Ji.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qu-yuan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qu Yuan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qu Yuan (c. 340–278 BCE) was a poet and minister of the State of Chu, traditionally regarded as the first great individual poet of China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quadragesimo-anno"><span class="enc-name">Quadragesimo Anno</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Quadragesimo Anno is an encyclical issued by Pope Pius XI on 15 May 1931, forty years after Leo XIII's Rerum Novarum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quakers"><span class="enc-name">Quakers</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quakers, or Religious Society of Friends, are a Christian movement founded by George Fox in England in the 1650s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quanah-parker"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quanah-parker.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quanah Parker</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quanah Parker was a war leader of the Kwahadi Comanche, the son of Peta Nocona and the captive Cynthia Ann Parker.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quang-binh-province"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quang-binh-province.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quảng Bình province</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quảng Bình is a historical province of north-central Vietnam, merged into Quảng Trị province in 2025.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quang-trung"><span class="enc-name">Quang Trung</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quang Trung, born Nguyen Hue, was the ablest of the three Tay Son brothers who rose against the Nguyen lords in 1771.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quanrong"><span class="enc-name">Quanrong</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quanrong were a people living on the western and north-western borders of the Zhou realm in early China, counted by Zhou writers among the non-Chinese Rong.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quanzhen-school"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quanzhen-school.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quanzhen School</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quanzhen (Complete Perfection) School is a branch of Daoism founded by Wang Chongyang in northern China under the Jin dynasty in the 1160s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quanzhou.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quanzhou</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quanzhou is a port city in Fujian province in south-eastern China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quba"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quba</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quba is a town in north-eastern Azerbaijan on the slopes of the Greater Caucasus, near the Caspian coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quchan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quchan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quchan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quchan is a town in Khorasan in north-eastern Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qudaa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qudaa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quda'a</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Quda'a was a large confederation of Arab tribes whose genealogists disputed whether it belonged to the northern or the southern Arabs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qudshanis"><span class="enc-name">Qudshanis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qudshanis is a village in Hakkâri Province in south-eastern Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qudsia-begum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qudsia-begum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qudsia Begum</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qudsia Begum was the first of the four women rulers of Bhopal State.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quebec"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quebec.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quebec</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quebec is a city on the St Lawrence River in Canada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quebec-act"><span class="enc-name">Quebec Act</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quebec Act was a British law of 1774 for the government of the Province of Quebec.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quechua"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quechua.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quechua</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Quechua is a family of languages spoken by some eight to ten million people in the Andes, chiefly in Peru, Bolivia and Ecuador.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg"><span class="enc-name">Quedlinburg</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quedlinburg is a town on the northern edge of the Harz in Saxony-Anhalt, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-amina"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/queen-amina.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Queen Amina</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amina is remembered in Hausa tradition as a warrior queen of Zazzau, in present-day northern Nigeria, usually placed in the 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Queen of Sheba</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">The Queen of Sheba is a figure from biblical and Quranic tradition who visited King Solomon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-shima"><span class="enc-name">Queen Shima</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Queen Shima was a ruler of Holing, or Kalingga, on the north coast of Java, said in the New Book of Tang to have been chosen as ruler in 674.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/queen-victoria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Queen Victoria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Victoria was queen of the United Kingdom from 1837 to 1901 and empress of India from 1876.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quelimane"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quelimane.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quelimane</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quelimane is a port town on the coast of Mozambique, near the Zambezi delta.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quemuenchatocha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quemuenchatocha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quemuenchatocha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quemuenchatocha was the zaque, ruler of the northern Muisca at Hunza (modern Tunja), when the Spanish arrived.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quentovic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quentovic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quentovic</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quentovic was a Frankish trading port near the mouth of the river Canche, close to modern Étaples in northern France, active from the seventh to the ninth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quercy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quercy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quercy</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quercy is a historic province of south-western France around Cahors, roughly the modern department of Lot and part of Tarn-et-Garonne.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/querfurt"><span class="enc-name">Querfurt</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Querfurt is a town in Saxony-Anhalt, Germany, west of Halle, dominated by one of the largest medieval castles in Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quetzalcoatl</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Quetzalcoatl, &quot;feathered serpent&quot;, is a Mesoamerican deity associated with wind, learning, priesthood and the morning star.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quetzaltenango</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quetzaltenango, called Xelajú or Xela, is the second-largest city of Guatemala, in the western highlands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qufu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qufu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qufu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qufu is a city in south-western Shandong, the capital of the State of Lu in the Zhou period and the birthplace of Confucius.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quhistan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quhistan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quhistan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quhistan, or Kuhistan, 'land of mountains', is a historical region of eastern Iran south of Khorasan, around the towns of Qa'in, Tun and Birjand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quiet-revolution"><span class="enc-name">Quiet Revolution</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quiet Revolution was the rapid secularisation and modernisation of Quebec in the 1960s, beginning with the election of Jean Lesage's Liberal government in 1960.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quimper"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quimper.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quimper</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quimper is a city in Finistère, western Brittany, at the confluence of the Odet and Steir rivers, and the historic capital of Cornouaille.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quinquereme"><span class="enc-name">Quinquereme</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A quinquereme was a large ancient warship powered by oarsmen arranged in groups of five per vertical unit.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintana-roo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quintana-roo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quintana Roo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintana Roo is a state of Mexico on the eastern side of the Yucatán Peninsula, along the Caribbean coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintipartite-deed"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quintipartite-deed.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quintipartite Deed</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quintipartite Deed of 1 July 1676 divided the English colony of New Jersey into East Jersey and West Jersey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-balearicus"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Balearicus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Balearicus was a Roman consul of 123 BCE who conquered the Balearic Islands, whose people were accused of harbouring pirates.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Creticus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Creticus was a Roman general and consul in 69 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-macedonicus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-macedonicus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Macedonicus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Macedonicus was a Roman general and statesman.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-numidicus"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Numidicus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Numidicus was a Roman general and leading conservative politician.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius was a Roman general and politician, a supporter of Sulla and consul with him in 80 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-curtius-rufus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quintus-curtius-rufus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Curtius Rufus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Curtius Rufus was a Roman historian, probably of the 1st century CE, author of the Histories of Alexander the Great in ten books, of which the first two are lost.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Fabius Maximus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Fabius Maximus was a Roman statesman and general during the Second Punic War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fulvius-flaccus-consul-179-bce"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Fulvius Flaccus (consul 179 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Fulvius Flaccus was a Roman general and politician who, as praetor in Hispania Citerior from 182 to 180 BCE, fought hard campaigns against the Celtiberians in…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fulvius-nobilior"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Fulvius Nobilior</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Fulvius Nobilior was a Roman consul in 153 BCE, the year when consuls first took office on 1 January so that he could leave early for the war in Hispania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-lollius-urbicus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quintus-lollius-urbicus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Lollius Urbicus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Lollius Urbicus was a Roman general from Numidia in North Africa who served as governor of Britain from about 139 to 142 under Antoninus Pius.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-marcius-philippus"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Marcius Philippus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Marcius Philippus was a Roman politician who was consul in 186 BCE and again in 169 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-opimius"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Opimius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Opimius was a Roman consul of 154 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-petillius-cerialis"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Petillius Cerialis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Petillius Cerialis was a Roman general and governor of Britain from 71 to 74 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-pleminius"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Pleminius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Pleminius was a Roman officer whom Scipio Africanus left in command of Locri after its recapture from Hannibal in 205 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-pompeius-consul-141-bce"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Pompeius (consul 141 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Pompeius was a Roman politician, the first of his family to reach the consulship, in 141 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-poppaedius-silo"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Poppaedius Silo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Poppaedius Silo was a leader of the Marsi and one of the two consuls of the Italian confederation of Italia in the Social War against Rome, which began in 91 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Sertorius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Sertorius was a Roman general of the Marian faction who, after Sulla's victory, made Hispania his base.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-servilius-caepio-consul-106-bce"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Servilius Caepio (consul 106 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Servilius Caepio was a Roman general and consul in 106 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-titurius-sabinus"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Titurius Sabinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Titurius Sabinus was a legate of Julius Caesar in the Gallic Wars.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quintus-tullius-cicero"><span class="enc-name">Quintus Tullius Cicero</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quintus Tullius Cicero was a Roman politician and soldier, the younger brother of the orator Marcus Tullius Cicero.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quit-india-movement"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quit-india-movement.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quit India Movement</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quit India Movement was a mass campaign launched by the Indian National Congress on 8 August 1942, demanding an immediate end to British rule.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quito"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quito.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quito</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quito is the capital of Ecuador, set in an Andean basin at about 2,850 metres just south of the equator.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quito-school"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quito-school.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quito School</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quito School (Escuela Quiteña) was a tradition of painting and sculpture that flourished in Quito during the 17th and 18th centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quitzow-family"><span class="enc-name">Quitzow family</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quitzow family was a noble family of the Prignitz in Brandenburg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quizquiz"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quizquiz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quizquiz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quizquiz was one of the leading generals of the Inca ruler Atahualpa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quli-qutb-mulk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quli-qutb-mulk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quli Qutb Mulk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Quli Qutb Mulk, known as Sultan Quli, was a Turkmen noble from Hamadan who became governor of Telangana for the Bahmani Sultanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qumbu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qumbu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qumbu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qumbu is a small town in the Eastern Cape province of South Africa, between Mthatha and Mount Frere.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qungrat"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qungrat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qungrat</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qungrat are a Turkic tribe of Central Asia, descended from the Mongol Qongirat of the 13th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quran"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quran.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quran</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quran is the central religious text of Islam, believed by Muslims to be the word of God revealed to the Prophet Muhammad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quraysh"><span class="enc-name">Quraysh</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quraysh were the dominant Arab tribe of Mecca, to which the Prophet Muhammad belonged.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qurchi-bashi"><span class="enc-name">Qurchi-bashi</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The qurchi-bashi was the commander of the qurchis, the royal guard of Qizilbash cavalry, in the Safavid Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qusayr-amra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qusayr-amra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qusayr Amra</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Qusayr Amra is a small Umayyad desert palace and bath-house in eastern Jordan, built in the early eighth century, probably for the prince al-Walid ibn Yazid, later the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qusayy-ibn-kilab"><span class="enc-name">Qusayy ibn Kilab</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qusayy ibn Kilab was, according to Arab tradition, the leader who gathered the scattered clans of the Quraysh in Mecca in the 5th century and won control of the Kaaba…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qustul"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qustul.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qustul</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qustul is an archaeological site in Lower Nubia, on the Nile near the modern border between Egypt and Sudan, now under Lake Nasser.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qut"><span class="enc-name">Qut</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Qut is an Old Turkic concept of heavenly charisma or good fortune, believed to be granted by the sky god Tengri to a legitimate ruler.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutalmish"><span class="enc-name">Qutalmish</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutalmish was a Seljuk prince, a cousin of Sultan Tughril, who took part in the Seljuk conquests in Iran and Iraq in the mid-eleventh century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim"><span class="enc-name">Qutayba ibn Muslim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutayba ibn Muslim was an Arab general and Umayyad governor of Khurasan from 705, appointed by al-Hajjaj.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutb-al-din-mawdud"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-al-din-mawdud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qutb al-Din Mawdud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutb al-Din Mawdud was the Zengid ruler of Mosul from 1149 to 1170, a son of Zengi and brother of Nur al-Din of Aleppo and Damascus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutb-minar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-minar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qutb Minar</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qutb Minar is a tall minaret of red sandstone in Delhi, begun around the end of the twelfth century under Qutb ud-Din Aibak and completed by his successors.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qutb Shahi dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qutb Shahi dynasty ruled the Golconda Sultanate in the Deccan from 1518 to 1687.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qutb ud-Din Aibak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutb ud-Din Aibak was a Turkic slave-general of the Ghurid ruler Muhammad of Ghor, who governed Delhi as viceroy from 1192.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-mubarak-shah"><span class="enc-name">Qutb ud-Din Mubarak Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutb ud-Din Mubarak Shah was the last sultan of the Khalji dynasty of Delhi, ruling from 1316 to 1320 after the death of his father Alauddin Khalji.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-khan-of-kasur"><span class="enc-name">Qutb-ud-din Khan of Kasur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutb-ud-din Khan was a Pathan ruler of Kasur in the Punjab in the early 19th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutlu-beg"><span class="enc-name">Qutlu Beg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutlu Beg was a chief of the Aq Qoyunlu Turkmen tribes of eastern Anatolia in the fourteenth century, son of Tur Ali Beg and father of Qara Yuluk Osman.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutlugh-bilge-kol-qaghan"><span class="enc-name">Qutlugh Bilge Kol Qaghan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutlugh Bilge Kol Qaghan was the founder of the Uyghur Khaganate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutlugh-khwaja"><span class="enc-name">Qutlugh Khwaja</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutlugh Khwaja was a Chagataid prince, a son of Duwa, who commanded the Chagatai forces in Afghanistan and led raids into the Delhi Sultanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutlugh-terken-khatun"><span class="enc-name">Qutlugh Terken Khatun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutlugh Terken Khatun was ruler of Kerman in southern Iran from 1257 to 1282, the widow of the Qutlugh-Khanid ruler Qutb al-Din Muhammad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutuz"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/qutuz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qutuz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qutuz was a sultan of the Mamluk Sultanate in 1259–1260.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/quwo-county"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/quwo-county.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Quwo County</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Quwo County is a county in Linfen, in southern Shanxi province, China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/quwwat-ul-islam-mosque"><span class="enc-name">Quwwat-ul-Islam Mosque</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Quwwat-ul-Islam Mosque is the earliest surviving mosque in Delhi, begun in the 1190s by Qutb al-Din Aybak after the Ghurid conquest, in what is now the Qutb complex.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qvarqvare-ii-jaqeli"><span class="enc-name">Qvarqvare II Jaqeli</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qvarqvare II Jaqeli was atabeg of Samtskhe, a principality in south-western Georgia, from 1451 to 1498.</span></a></li></ul>
